"""
The 12 V auxiliaries as a constant power drawn from the traction battery's DC link: the DC-DC
converter and everything behind it (control units, pumps, fans, lights), lumped into one draw
`P_aux` (deck 7's O_aux).

    T di/dt = P_aux / v - i

`i` is the current drawn, into `p` and out of `n`. The DC-DC converter's control holds its draw
at `P_aux` with the time constant `T`, so in steady state `v i = P_aux` and the current rises as
the link voltage sags. The DC-DC converter's own loss is inside `P_aux`.

The current is a state starting at zero, not solved from `v i = P_aux`: a constant power
behind a source resistance has two operating points, the link near its open voltage with a
small current and the link collapsed with a huge one (0.086 V and 3500 A on the Leaf pack), and
initialization of the algebraic form can pick the collapsed one.

The default is for a car driven on a test cycle with the climate control off: 300 W (assumed).
Lecture 2's road-load energy notebook used 500 W (assumed) for the engine car's auxiliaries.
"""
component AuxLoad
  extends ElectricalComponents.Analog.Interfaces.OnePort
  "Power drawn by the 12 V network through the DC-DC converter (assumed)"
  parameter P_aux::Power = 300.0
  "Time constant of the DC-DC converter's power control (assumed)"
  parameter T::Time = 0.01
relations
  initial i = 0.0
  T * der(i) = P_aux / v - i
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://VehicleSystemsComponents/Vehicle/Electric/AuxLoad.svg"}}
}
end
